--TEST--
A call through a `mixed` receiver defers to the receiver's runtime class, and refuses the three spellings that deferral cannot express — `rule:types/conversion`, `rule:types/erased-member-access`, `rule:types/callable-values`
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Box {
    public function value(int $n): string { return "v"; }
}

// Accepted, and written first so that a spelling that stops deferring fails
// here rather than as a missing refusal. `rule:types/conversion` makes `mixed` the one
// unchecked position, so `rule:types/erased-member-access`'s deferral covers a call as well as a
// property access: an ordinary positional call is answered by the receiver's
// own descriptor when it runs, and so is a `...`, whose count is its own
// run-time length rather than anything this site resolved.
mixed $m = new Box();
$m->value(1);

array<mixed> $rest = [1];
$m->value(...$rest);

// A `name:` argument has nothing to resolve against, exactly as through a
// `callable`: the method row that marshals the call carries the callee's
// arity and its parameter tags, never their names.
$m->value(n: 1);

// An `inout` parameter list is packed and written back at the call site,
// which a call that learns its callee at run time cannot do — the limit ADR
// 0043 § 4's synthesized forward already names.
int $n = 1;
$m->value(inout $n);

// A method reference (`rule:types/callable-values`) makes no call at all: it
// names a callable, which carries its callee with it, and there is no class
// here to read one off.
$m->value(...);
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0712]: `n:` names no parameter of a call through a `mixed` receiver
   --> %s:21:11
   |
21 | $m->value(n: 1);
   |           ^^^^ written by name here
   = help: `rule:types/erased-member-access`: the receiver's runtime class chooses the callee, and the method row that marshals the call carries its arity and its parameter tags rather than their names — pass the argument positionally, or narrow the receiver to the class that declares the member

error[E0714]: `inout` names no parameter of a call through a `mixed` receiver
   --> %s:27:11
   |
27 | $m->value(inout $n);
   |           ^^^^^^^^ marked `inout` here
   = help: `rule:types/erased-member-access` defers this call to the receiver's runtime class, and an `inout` parameter list is packed and written back here at the call site — so a callee that is not known until the call runs can never bind one; narrow the receiver with `is` or `as ClassName` if the write-back is what was meant

error[E0732]: `value(...)` cannot be a method reference on a `mixed` value
   --> %s:32:1
   |
32 | $m->value(...);
   | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ a method reference is made here
   = help: a call on a `mixed` value finds its method when the program runs. A method reference needs the class when the program compiles, and there is no class here. Call the method directly (`$m->value(…)`), or narrow the value first with `is` or `as ClassName`

error: aborting due to 3 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
